WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:06.467
Startled, I turned around to see who had spoken to us in French. It was the captain of the vessel!

2
00:00:06.468 --> 00:00:10.705
Ned immediately released the steward, who tottered out of the cabin.

3
00:00:10.706 --> 00:00:16.618
Leaning against the corner of the table, the captain folded his arms and studied us with great care.

4
00:00:16.619 --> 00:00:20.777
Did he regret the words that he had just spoken? I wondered.

5
00:00:20.778 --> 00:00:25.110
For several moments there was silence, and none of us dared to break it.

6
00:00:25.111 --> 00:00:33.943
"Gentlemen," the captain said in a calm and penetrating voice, "I am fluent in French, English, German, and Latin.

7
00:00:33.944 --> 00:00:39.531
Therefore I could have answered you earlier, but I wanted time to reflect.

8
00:00:39.532 --> 00:00:44.594
Each one of you told essentially the same story, so I believe you were telling the truth.

9
00:00:44.595 --> 00:00:58.673
I now know that you are Professor Pierre Aronnax of the Paris Museum of Natural History, his servant Conseil, and Ned Land, a Canadian harpooner aboard the U.S. Navy warship Abraham Lincoln

10
00:00:58.674 --> 00:01:01.753
." I bowed slightly to show that he was

11
00:01:01.754 --> 00:01:10.086
correct. The captain spoke French with perfect ease and without a trace of a foreign accent. Yet he didn't seem to be from

12
00:01:10.087 --> 00:01:16.078
France. "No doubt you think that I've delayed a long time in paying you this second visit," the captain continued.

13
00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:19.283
"But I needed time to ponder my course of action toward you.

14
00:01:19.284 --> 00:01:27.237
Chance has brought you into the presence of a man who has cut all ties with humanity. Your arrival has disrupted my whole existence."

15
00:01:27.238 --> 00:01:29.817
"Unintentionally!" I replied.

16
00:01:29.818 --> 00:01:33.740
"Unintentionally?" replied the captain, raising his voice a little.

17
00:01:33.741 --> 00:01:38.483
"Was it unintentionally that the Abraham Lincoln chased me all over the Pacific?

18
00:01:38.484 --> 00:01:42.475
Was it unintentionally that you became a passenger on that warship?

19
00:01:42.476 --> 00:01:47.054
Was it unintentionally that your cannon balls rebounded off my vessel?

20
00:01:47.055 --> 00:01:52.062
Was it unintentionally that Ned Land struck it with his harpoon?"

21
00:01:52.063 --> 00:01:57.634
I detected a restrained irritation in these words but knew that I had to respond.

22
00:01:57.635 --> 00:02:05.126
"Sir, you are probably unaware that everyone in Europe and America is talking about your vessel," I said.

23
00:02:05.127 --> 00:02:14.785
"In pursuing you, those onboard the Abraham Lincoln believed that they were chasing a sea creature, a horrible monster that they had to destroy at any price."

24
00:02:14.786 --> 00:02:18.039
The captain was not impressed with my explanation.

25
00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:23.848
"I'm sure that your warship would have been just as happy to destroy a submarine," he said.

26
00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:27.689
I'm afraid that I agreed with him, but I didn't say that aloud.

27
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:31.769
"Therefore," he continued, "I'm within my rights to treat you as enemies.

28
00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:38.268
I could place you on top of this vessel and then sink below the waves, forgetting you ever existed."

29
00:02:38.269 --> 00:02:42.427
"No civilized man would do that!" I protested.

30
00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:52.911
"I'm not a civilized man. I have finished with society, for reasons that I alone know. Therefore I do not obey society's laws."

31
00:02:52.912 --> 00:03:01.324
A flash of anger kindled in his eyes, and I had a glimpse of his terrible past. After a rather long silence, he went on.

32
00:03:01.325 --> 00:03:05.923
"I've decided that there's a way to reconcile my pity for you with my own interests.

33
00:03:05.924 --> 00:03:09.582
You will remain onboard my vessel since fate has cast you here.

34
00:03:09.583 --> 00:03:17.721
You will be free, but in exchange for this liberty, I must impose one condition and you must swear to agree to it."

35
00:03:17.722 --> 00:03:20.316
"What is that?" I asked.

36
00:03:20.317 --> 00:03:27.554
"From time to time, I may have to confine you to your cabins for hours or even days," said the captain.

37
00:03:27.555 --> 00:03:32.801
"As I never want to use violence, I expect you to obey. Do you accept this condition?"

38
00:03:32.802 --> 00:03:39.292
"We do," I replied. "But what do you mean when you say that we'll be free?"

39
00:03:39.293 --> 00:03:42.371
"You'll be allowed to freely wander the submarine." It

40
00:03:42.372 --> 00:03:48.617
dawned on me what this really meant. "Do you mean that we'll never see our friends and families again?" "Yes,

41
00:03:48.618 --> 00:03:50.936
sir," replied the captain. "What!"

42
00:03:50.937 --> 00:03:55.849
exclaimed Ned. "I can't swear that I'll never try to escape." "But

43
00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:58.666
this is cruelty!" I cried. "No,

44
00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:06.115
it's compassion," said the captain. "You are my prisoners of war, and I could throw you into the ocean depths if I wanted to. You

45
00:04:06.116 --> 00:04:10.796
attacked me and inadvertently found the secret of my whole existence. Do

46
00:04:10.797 --> 00:04:14.758
you think I'm going to send you back into the world with that secret? Never! In

47
00:04:14.759 --> 00:04:19.083
restraining you, it's not you that I guard but myself." "So

48
00:04:19.084 --> 00:04:24.417
you are basically giving us a choice between life and death?" I asked. "Yes."

49
00:04:24.418 --> 00:04:31.163
But then he went on in a gentler tone, "I know your work, Professor Aronnax. Your

50
00:04:31.164 --> 00:04:35.393
book on the mysteries of the seas is one of my favorites. You've

51
00:04:35.394 --> 00:04:40.671
done as good a job as you could from land, but you haven't seen nearly enough. You

52
00:04:40.672 --> 00:04:47.742
won't regret your time onboard my vessel because I'm about to embark on an underwater tour of the world." I

53
00:04:47.743 --> 00:04:53.052
have to admit that this intrigued me, and for a moment I forgot that we were prisoners. "By

54
00:04:53.053 --> 00:04:56.456
what name should we address you?" I asked. "To

55
00:04:56.457 --> 00:05:04.226
you, I'm just Captain Nemo, and to me, you're just passengers on my submarine, the Nautilus ."

56
00:05:04.227 --> 00:05:07.035
I smiled when the captain gave his name.

57
00:05:07.036 --> 00:05:13.361
Obviously he was intent on keeping his identity secret because nemo means "nobody" in Latin.

58
00:05:13.362 --> 00:05:16.131
Captain Nemo called and a steward appeared.

59
00:05:16.132 --> 00:05:21.528
The captain gave the steward his orders in that strange language that I couldn't understand.

60
00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:24.718
Then he turned to Ned and Conseil.

61
00:05:24.719 --> 00:05:29.353
"Follow the steward to your cabin, where breakfast is waiting," said Captain Nemo.

62
00:05:29.354 --> 00:05:35.337
"And now, Professor Aronnax, our meal is also ready. Permit me to show you the way."

63
00:05:35.338 --> 00:05:39.258
He led me through a passage until we came to an elegant dining room.

64
00:05:39.259 --> 00:05:47.417
Tall oak cabinets stood at both ends of the room; their shelves were filled with china and glass of inestimable value.

65
00:05:47.418 --> 00:05:54.179
In the center of the room was a table spread with a fine meal. Captain Nemo indicated where I should sit.

66
00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:00.742
I recognized some of the foods at this breakfast but not all of them. Captain Nemo seemed to read my thoughts.

67
00:06:00.743 --> 00:06:07.496
"Most of these dishes are unknown to you," he said, "but they all come from the sea and you may eat them without fear. For

68
00:06:07.497 --> 00:06:12.925
a long time my crew and I have renounced food from the land, and we are healthy." "Then

69
00:06:12.926 --> 00:06:17.242
where did you get this meat?" I pointed to one of the dishes. "That

70
00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:21.242
which you believe to be meat, Professor, is really fillet of turtle. My

71
00:06:21.243 --> 00:06:25.805
chef is a clever fellow who excels at cooking the various products of the sea." I

72
00:06:25.806 --> 00:06:30.234
ate while Captain Nemo entertained me with extraordinary stories. "You

73
00:06:30.235 --> 00:06:33.147
like the sea, Captain?" I asked. "Yes,

74
00:06:33.148 --> 00:06:36.404
I love it! The sea doesn't belong to tyrants. Upon

75
00:06:36.405 --> 00:06:42.350
its surface men can still wage war, but at thirty feet below the surface, their power disappears. Here

76
00:06:42.351 --> 00:06:46.466
I recognize no masters and I am free!" Captain

77
00:06:46.467 --> 00:06:53.712
Nemo suddenly fell silent in the midst of his enthusiastic outpouring. Did he feel that he had said too much? For

78
00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:57.704
a few moments he paced up and down, much agitated. Then

79
00:06:57.705 --> 00:07:03.123
he grew calmer and his face recovered its usual icy composure. "Now,

80
00:07:03.131 --> 00:07:10.988
Professor," he said, turning to me, "if you'd like to inspect the Nautilus , come this way."

